From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 08:33:05 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: report the codec resources a decoder holds

A hardware video decoder runs Android's codec availability tests, which
read what the device has left from the store's resource capacity and from
what every instance says it needs. The store reported the resources of
the encoders only, and a decoder reported none, so the framework could
not tell what a decoder took and the tests failed in their prerequisite.

A decoder now reports one session and, when the product states a decode
pixel rate, the pixel rate of its stream in real time, as the encoders
do: it follows the frame rate, the operating rate and the priority the
client sets, reports a change with its next output, and claims its pixel
rate when it starts, failing the start where the device's is used up. The
decoders have a pixel rate of their own, because the device sustains a
different one than for the encoders: the store reports it from
ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.video_decode_pixel_rate, and the pixel rate budget
and the claim are shared code that a component of either direction uses.

The need follows the size of the pictures the device decodes, not the
size the client configured: a stream that changes its size, as adaptive
playback does, changes what the instance needs, and the framework learns
of it with the next output.

The picture size parameter is stepped by 2, the alignment the codec XML
publishes, and the decoded size is rounded up to that, which leaves the size
of any 4:2:0 stream as it is. A larger step rounds the 360 rows of a 480x360
stream up to 368, and the framework, which reads the parameter again after a
flush, reports a second output format change that the stream does not have.
